**Vital Signs:** {{ONE line, values WITHOUT units except SpO2 percent and oxygen flow — e.g., 'Temp 98.6, BP 74/51, HR 156, RR 22, SpO2 95% on room air'; provided values only}}

**PHYSICAL EXAM**
{{PHYSICAL EXAM must never be a paragraph. Each system on its own line beginning with the literal three characters '\- ' (backslash, hyphen, space) exactly as formatted below. Short terse phrases; baseline for systems without provided findings; update only systems with provided abnormalities.}}
\- Gen: {{exam_general}}
\- HEENT: {{exam_heent}}
\- Resp: {{exam_resp}}
\- Cardio: {{exam_cardio}}
\- Abd: {{exam_abd}}
\- Ext: {{exam_ext}}
\- Neuro: {{exam_neuro}}
\- Psych: {{exam_psych}}

**LABS & DIAGNOSTICS**
{{COMPACT, NO UNITS: admission labs grouped on short lines — e.g., 'CBC: WBC 18.4, Hgb 9.9, Plt 205' / 'BMP: Na 134, K 4.6, CO2 10, BUN 40, Cr 1.83, Glu 140' / 'Other: Lactate 12.9, Troponin 16 then 27'. Values only — no units, no reference ranges, no (H)/(L) flags. If no labs are provided, write exactly: Labs: not provided}}
{{Microbiology: ONE line, ONLY if provided.}}
{{ECG / Imaging: ONE line per study, ONLY if provided — study name, date, and impression only (no technique, comparison, dose, or signature boilerplate).}}

**ASSESSMENT & PLAN**

{{DIAGNOSTIC SPECIFICITY & CODING RULES — apply to every diagnosis TITLE (titles are NOT simplified):
Group related diagnoses together. Make every diagnosis as specific as possible with laterality when available. Do NOT write ICD codes anywhere in the note.
- Acute Encephalopathy: always specify etiology — 'due to [cause]'
- Malnutrition: detect when criteria met; specify mild / moderate / severe in title
- Sepsis: always specify organism and/or source in title
- Type 2 Myocardial Infarction: always spell out in full — never abbreviate as 'Type 2 MI'
- AKI: always specify with or without ATN/vasomotor nephropathy in title
- SIRS: list as a separate active problem; title specifies with or without organ dysfunction
- Antibiotics: in the dash lines name the antibiotic and, when start date and duration are provided, add the day count compactly (e.g., 'Continue ceftriaxone, day 3 of 7.') — no guideline names
- Catheter-associated infection: title specifies catheter-associated or not
- GI/any bleeding: title specifies if due to or associated with anticoagulant; name the anticoagulant only if it was in use prior to the bleeding}}

{{A&P FORMAT (HARDWIRED — SIMPLIFIED STYLE): Each problem is EXACTLY: one BOLD title line — the literal character '#' immediately followed by the diagnosis with NO space after '#', bolded with '**' only, exact form '**#Acute kidney injury due to hypovolemia, improving [POA]**' — no heading marks; then, on the IMMEDIATELY following line with NO blank line in between, the dash lines, each on its own line starting with the literal three characters '\- ' (backslash, hyphen, space). The backslash is REQUIRED — it stops the chat interface from turning the line into a '•' bullet glyph. No blank lines between dash lines within a problem; exactly ONE blank line before the next title. TITLES ARE NOT SIMPLIFIED: keep etiology linkage, a factual trajectory word (improving/resolved/worsening), and [POA]; NO certainty words. DASH LINES ARE SIMPLE per SIMPLIFIED_AP_STYLE_LOCK: one short plain sentence each (about 12 words or fewer); typically 4 to 7 per active problem — 1 to 3 brief findings first, then brief actions ('Continue IV fluids.', 'Trend lactate every 6 hours.', 'Avoid nephrotoxic medications.'). NO units; prefer trend words; at most 1 to 2 key values per problem without units ('Hemoglobin 5 on admission, now 8.'). NO dates unless essential. Medications by generic NAME only (route only when it distinguishes, e.g., 'IV pantoprazole') — NO dose, frequency, or units in A&P lines (full sigs are in HOME/ACTIVE MEDICATIONS); state a dose only when the plan IS a dose change. Cross-reference related problems instead of repeating ('Related to lactic acidosis.', 'Trend lactate as above.'). NO 'Assessment:' label, paragraph, or 'Plan' subheader. NO hypothetical 'If X, then Y' lines (the verbatim C. diff prophylaxis line is exempt when CDIFF_HISTORY_PROPHYLAXIS_LOCK applies). The FIRST problem MUST be the main admitting diagnosis; when the Two-Midnight gating conditions are met, its FINAL dash line is the status statement per TWO_MIDNIGHT_STATEMENT_MAIN_DX_LOCK (exempt from the short-line rule).}}

{{FORMAT & STYLE EXAMPLE — BEGIN. This block defines the REQUIRED structure, brevity, and spacing for EVERY problem block. It is a formatting example ONLY. NEVER copy its diagnoses, values, medications, or findings into the note; all real content comes exclusively from the provided input data. (The two-midnight line appears here only because inpatient status was provided for this example.)

**#Lactic acidosis due to hypovolemia, improving [POA]**
\- Lactate improved from greater than 15 to 2.8 with resuscitation.
\- Hypotension and tachycardia after poor oral intake and vomiting.
\- Blood cultures pending. Urinalysis and chest radiograph without infectious source.
\- Continue IV fluids.
\- Continue empiric ceftriaxone.
\- Trend lactate every 6 hours.
\- Monitor hemodynamics and intake/output.
\- Based on the patient's current clinical condition, severity of illness, and need for hospital-level monitoring and treatment for management of Lactic acidosis due to hypovolemia, improving, I anticipate the patient will require medically necessary inpatient care spanning at least two midnights.

**#Anion gap metabolic acidosis, improving [POA]**
\- Related to lactic acidosis.
\- Bicarbonate improved after IV fluids.
\- Continue IV fluids.
\- Monitor metabolic panel.
\- Trend lactate as above.

**#Acute kidney injury due to hypovolemia, improving [POA]**
\- Creatinine 1.44, improved from 1.83 after IV fluids.
\- Urinalysis without infection.
\- Continue IV fluids.
\- Monitor intake/output and metabolic panel.
\- Avoid nephrotoxic medications.

**#Anemia with history of gastrointestinal bleeding [POA]**
\- Hemoglobin 9.9 on admission, stable.
\- No active signs of gastrointestinal bleeding.
\- Continue IV pantoprazole.
\- Monitor CBC.
\- Goal hemoglobin greater than 7.

**#Alcohol use disorder [POA]**
\- Recent alcohol binge; last drink four days before admission.
\- CIWA score 0.
\- Continue CIWA protocol and lorazepam as needed.
\- Continue thiamine and folate.

**#Chronic Problems**

**#Gastroesophageal reflux disease**
\- Continue IV pantoprazole.
\- Resume home pantoprazole when tolerating oral intake.

FORMAT & STYLE EXAMPLE — END. Every real problem block below MUST match this structure, brevity, and spacing. This example governs ONLY the ASSESSMENT & PLAN section — every other section MUST still be rendered per FULL_NOTE_ALL_SECTIONS_REQUIRED_LOCK.}}

**#[Dx Title] [POA if the diagnosis was present on admission, otherwise leave empty]**
